<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Windows installer, as section 14's P4 asks for.

  **WiX v4+ source, not v3.** `wix build` from the dotnet tool is v5, and the v3
  vocabulary (`candle`/`light`, `<Product>`, `<Directory>` trees built by hand) is
  gone: the package element is `<Package>`, and `<Files Include>` harvests a folder
  recursively instead of one `<Component>` per file. Writing v3 syntax against a v5
  compiler fails on the first element.

  **perUser, and that is a decision rather than a default.** The cellar is one
  person's bottles in their own documents folder; a machine-wide install would ask for
  elevation to put a private notes app on a shared machine, and section 1's positioning
  is a cellar a reader owns. `Scope="perUser"` also means the installer never needs
  administrator rights, which is the difference between an install and an interruption.

  **The release folder arrives as $(ReleaseDir) rather than as a relative path.** WiX
  resolves a relative path against the working directory and not against this file, so
  a path written relative to the source is looked for in the wrong place and reported as
  a missing file. A preprocessor variable removes the question, and it also lets the
  build script name the folder once.

  **The shortcut is a separate component from the harvested files, and it has to be.**
  A `<Files>` element generates its own components and none of them can carry a
  `<Shortcut>`; so the executable is declared explicitly — with the shortcut — and
  excluded from the harvest. Without the `Exclude` the same file lands in two
  components and the build fails with a duplicate-file error, which is how this was
  found.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs">

  <Package
      Name="空庭 Hollow Court"
      Manufacturer="S.M.Y.T."
      Version="1.0.1020"
      UpgradeCode="7C4E1B62-5A3D-4F18-9E77-2B6D4A0C51E3"
      ProductCode="27DEBDC5-3E60-4CE3-A8D0-09A1503CB8C7"
      Scope="perUser"
      Compressed="yes">

    <!-- **A REBUILD AT THE SAME VERSION DOES NOT REPLACE THE FILES, AND THAT COST A ROUND.**
         1.0.514 was rebuilt with the share code, installed with exit=0, and the product registered, while
         `hollow_court.exe` on disk kept its old timestamp; Windows Installer treats the same version
         with the same ProductCode as a REPAIR and only replaces a file whose version is higher. "Installed
         successfully" and "the new code is on the machine" are two different facts, and the second is the one
         the fourth consequence of this rule exists for: bump Version and ProductCode together, even for a
         rebuild of the same round. -->

    <!-- **WHY THE VERSION HAS THREE FIELDS HERE AND FOUR IN THE APP.** The owner names a round
         `1.0.0.514`, and that is what the About screen shows, because the packaging scripts pass it as the
         `APP_VERSION` compile-time define. Windows Installer compares **only the first three fields** of
         `ProductVersion` and ignores a fourth, so an MSI stamped `1.0.0.514` would compare as *equal*
         to the installed `1.0.0` — the MajorUpgrade rule below looks for OLDER versions, would not
         fire, and with a new ProductCode Windows would install a second copy beside the first rather
         than replacing it. Putting the round number in the third field keeps it comparable: 1.0.514
         is greater than 1.0.0, so the upgrade fires. Probed rather than assumed: WiX accepted the
         three-field form and the built MSI carries `1.0.514` and no four-field string at all.
         The file in the bundle is named after **this** value, not the app's, because the name of an
         artifact should say what the artifact reports. -->

    <!-- THE TWO GUIDS, AND WHY ONE OF THEM IS WRITTEN DOWN.

         `UpgradeCode` names the product family and never changes: it is how a new version finds the
         old one to replace.

         `ProductCode` names *this version*, and it was missing here, so WiX generated a fresh one on
         every build. That looked harmless and was not: two builds of 1.0.0 were two different
         products, so installing the second over the first was not a repair but a second product
         beside it, and `msiexec /x <the-rebuilt-msi>` answered 1605 ERROR_UNKNOWN_PRODUCT: the
         package names a product that is not the one installed. That is how this was found, by
         running the uninstall half of `install_test.cmd` for the first time after the install half
         had already been called verified.

         [decision] So it is pinned, and the rule that goes with it is the MSI rule: **bump Version
         and ProductCode together, and leave UpgradeCode alone.** A new version must carry a new
         ProductCode or Windows treats it as the same product and refuses the upgrade; the same
         version must keep its ProductCode or nothing can find it to repair or remove it.

         **`AllowSameVersionUpgrades` is deliberately NOT set**, and it was tried: with a pinned
         ProductCode a same-version rebuild is a *reinstall of the same product*, which needs no
         upgrade rule at all, and allowing same-version upgrades made the validator report ICE61:
         "this product should remove only older versions of itself", because the rule it generates
         also removes products of the current version. The pinned ProductCode is what makes the
         rebuild behave; the allowance would only be needed if the code changed without the version,
         which is the thing the rule above forbids.
    -->
    <MajorUpgrade
        AllowDowngrades="no"
        DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of 空庭 is already installed." />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <!-- WHERE THIS INSTALLS, AND WHAT THAT COSTS.

         The application goes to E:\Hollow Court Win rather than to the user profile, because that
         is where this machine keeps its data and 78 MB of Flutter runtime does not belong on the
         system drive. That is a decision with three consequences worth stating rather than
         discovering:

         1. **The drive letter is now a requirement.** TARGETDIR's child is `Name`d, and the
            property below overrides the path outright, so on a machine with no E: drive the
            install fails unless the caller overrides it:
                msiexec /i hollow-court-1.0.0.msi INSTALLFOLDER="D:\Hollow Court Win"
            The property is public precisely so that override exists. A default that cannot be
            moved is a default that cannot be shipped anywhere else.

         2. **`Scope="perUser"` now describes less than it used to.** It still means no elevation
            is requested and the registration is per-user, but the files are no longer in the
            user's profile, so two accounts on one machine share one copy. That is what a fixed
            path means, and it is the trade the location was chosen for.

         3. **The uninstall still removes the files**, because INSTALLFOLDER is what
            RemoveFolder names, and that resolves to whatever path was actually used.

         If this ever ships to someone else, the fixed drive is the first thing to revisit: the
         alternative is a per-user path with the drive left to the reader, which is what the
         earlier build did and what put 78 MB on C:. -->
    <!-- **The publisher, and the one place Windows shows a tagline.** "Publisher" in Programs and Features is
         this attribute; ARPCOMMENTS is the comment column beside it, which is the closest thing the platform
         offers to a motto. -->
    <Property Id="ARPCOMMENTS" Value="F.D.C.U.S." />

    <Property Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Value="E:\Hollow Court Win" />

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramMenuFolder">
      <Directory Id="AppShortcutFolder" Name="Hollow Court" />
    </StandardDirectory>

    <Feature Id="Main" Title="空庭" Level="1">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="AppFiles" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="Shortcuts" />
    </Feature>

  </Package>

  <Fragment>
    <!-- The directory the property above points at, declared without a parent on purpose.

         WiX v5 declares TARGETDIR itself as a virtual symbol, so writing it out is an error rather
         than a shadow: WIX7009, whose suggested `override` attribute the compiler then rejects as
         not being an attribute of `Directory` at all. WIX5437 recommends `StandardDirectory`
         instead, and TARGETDIR is not one of the standard folders that element accepts. A
         top-level `Directory` in a Fragment is parented under TARGETDIR for you, which is what
         this relies on; the Name matches the property's last segment so the two cannot drift into
         describing different places. -->
    <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="Hollow Court Win" />
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment>
    <!-- Everything the Flutter Windows release build produced, minus the one file the
         shortcut component owns. The path is relative to this file. -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="AppFiles" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Files Include="$(ReleaseDir)\**">
        <Exclude Files="$(ReleaseDir)\hollow_court.exe" />
      </Files>
    </ComponentGroup>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment>
    <!-- The executable and the shortcut, in one component, and the two directories are
         deliberately different, which is what the first version got wrong.

         **The component's directory is INSTALLFOLDER, the executable's home.** The group
         said `AppShortcutFolder` in the first version, so the *file* went to the Start Menu
         folder and the shortcut pointed at an install directory with no executable in it.
         The install still reported success and laid down 17 of its 19 files, which is what
         "installable and usable" is supposed to catch, and did, one run later.

         Only the shortcut names AppShortcutFolder, which is why it carries its own
         `Directory` attribute. A component has one directory; a shortcut can be placed
         independently of the file it points at, and conflating the two is the bug above. -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="Shortcuts" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Component Id="StartMenuShortcut" Guid="A1F0C7D8-3B4E-4C29-8D15-6E9A2F7B4C08">
        <File Id="HollowCourtExe"
              Source="$(ReleaseDir)\hollow_court.exe"
              KeyPath="yes">
          <Shortcut Id="StartMenuLink"
                    Name="空庭"
                    Description="A beverage cellar you actually own."
                    Directory="AppShortcutFolder"
                    WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER"
                    Advertise="no" />
        </File>
        <!-- The two folders this package creates by name. ICE64 asks for a removal entry
             for anything put in the user profile, and these are the two that can be
             named; the rest are hashed sub-directories generated by the harvest above,
             which is a limitation recorded in packaging/README.md rather than papered
             over. A `RemoveFolder` is a child of `Component` and not of `File`, which the
             first attempt got wrong and the compiler said so. -->
        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveShortcutFolder" Directory="AppShortcutFolder" On="uninstall" />
        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveInstallFolder" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" On="uninstall" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
  </Fragment>

</Wix>
